2. The Capability State Space

2.1 Core Definitions

Let $\mathcal{X} \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ denote the cognitive state space. The state vector at time $t$ is

\mathbf{C}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} K(t) \\ R(t) \\ M(t) \\ X(t) \\ E(t) \\ B(t) \\ A(t) \end{bmatrix} \in \mathcal{X},

where $K$ is knowledge, $R$ reasoning, $M$ metacognitive monitoring, $X$ exploratory capacity, $E$ epistemic calibration, $B$ trust calibration, and $A$ autonomous agency. Each component is grounded in established cognitive constructs.

The task space $\mathcal{T}$ is parameterized by difficulty $d \in [0,\infty)$ and domain $\omega \in \Omega$; capability is task-indexed, $\mathbf{C}:\mathcal{T}\to\mathcal{X}$, but task dependence is often suppressed for notational clarity.

2.2 Coupling States

We distinguish three capability vectors:

· Human-native capability $\mathbf{C}_H(t)$: performance without AI assistance.
· AI-assisted capability $\mathbf{C}_{HA}(t)$: performance with AI cooperation; we assume $\mathbf{C}_{HA}(t) \succeq \mathbf{C}_H(t)$.
· Internalized capability $\mathbf{C}_I(t)$: capability retained after AI removal.

The relationship between $\mathbf{C}_H$ and $\mathbf{C}_I$ is given by the internalization–autonomization mapping $\mathcal{F}$, defined through the flow of the generative vector field.

2.3 Capability Norm

We define the weighted norm

\|\mathbf{C}\|_{\mathbf{w}} = \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i |C_i|^p \right)^{1/p}, \quad p \geq 1, \ \mathbf{w} \succ 0.

The weight vector encodes normative judgments about which capabilities matter; it is not prescribed by the framework. This is critical: the optimal control formulation that follows is conditional on this normative choice.

