5. Beneficial Cognitive Coupling

5.1 Reversibility

Define the reversibility function

R_C = \frac{\|\mathbf{C}_H(t_1)\|}{\|\mathbf{C}_H(t_0)\|},

where $t_0$ is before AI interaction and $t_1$ is after AI removal. Coupling is reversible if $R_C \ge 1$, partially reversible if $R_C \in [R_{min},1)$, and irreversible otherwise.

If after AI removal the system evolves under the human-native decay $\dot{\mathbf{C}} = -\delta \mathbf{C}$, then the Lyapunov function $V(\mathbf{C}) = \frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{C} - \mathbf{C}_0\|^2$ satisfies $\dot V \le 0$ if and only if the interaction was reversible.

5.2 Beneficial Coupling Set

Definition 5.1 (Beneficial Cognitive Coupling). A trajectory of the coupled system is beneficially coupled over $[t_0,t_1]$ if:

\|\mathbf{C}(t_1)\| > \|\mathbf{C}(t_0)\|,

D(t) < D_{max} \quad \forall t \in [t_0,t_1],

E(t) > E_{min} \quad \forall t \in [t_0,t_1],

R_C \ge 1.

The set of states satisfying these conditions is denoted $\mathcal{B}$ (the Beneficial Coupling Set).

Remark: The term "cognitive symbiosis" carries strong biological metaphorical meaning. The formal object is the set $\mathcal{B}$, and philosophical discussion of symbiosis can reference this set without requiring biological analogy.

