11. Relationship to Empirical Literature

Our framework is consistent with recent findings:

· CentaurBench demonstrates that assistance is not reliably beneficial and that autonomy-optimal models are not necessarily assistance-optimal. This supports our distinction between $\mathbf{C}_{HA}$ and $\mathbf{C}_H$.
· AIRIS frames epistemic agency preservation as a control-allocation problem, overlapping with our verification and metacognition variables.
· Cognitive virus models address dependence at the population level. Our theory occupies the complementary individual-level scale.
· Speedup illusion studies show that subjective effort reduction does not imply objective capability growth, reinforcing our claim that $\mathbf{C}_{HA} \uparrow \not\Rightarrow \mathbf{C}_H \uparrow$.

