13. Conclusion

This dissertation has developed a mathematical framework for characterizing beneficial human–LLM coupling. The architecture is internally specified, with the principal deterministic, optimal-control, stochastic, observability, and computational components formally defined.

The central insight is that observed AI-assisted performance can increase while latent human capability decreases. This distinction between augmentation and amplification motivates a control problem fundamentally different from conventional AI optimization.

The mathematical status of each result is explicitly classified. Several results are established conditionally under explicit assumptions. Stronger claims concerning positivity, global bifurcation structure, stochastic discontinuous dynamics, fading optimality, and empirical parameterization remain subjects of theorem strengthening or experimental validation.

The framework generates falsifiable predictions and a validation protocol. The deepest hypothesis remains that intelligence is distributed, and the scientific challenge is to understand the dynamics of that distribution—not merely to optimize immediate performance, but to design coupling regimes that amplify human cognitive capacity without eroding it.

The mathematical architecture presented here provides the language for that design. The empirical work of parameter estimation, validation, and refinement begins now.

