Appendix D: Observability and State Estimation

D.1 Observation Model

The observations are

\mathbf{Y}(t) = \mathbf{H}(\mathbf{X}(t)) + \eta(t),

where $\eta(t)$ is measurement noise with covariance $\mathbf{R}$.

D.2 Nonlinear Local Observability

For nonlinear systems, local weak observability at $\mathbf{X}^*$ is characterized by the observability codistribution:

\mathcal{O}(x) = \operatorname{span} \left\{ d h_i, \, d L_f h_i, \, d L_f^2 h_i, \ldots \right\},

where $h_i$ are the components of $\mathbf{H}$ and $L_f$ denotes the Lie derivative along the vector field $\mathbf{F}$. The condition is

\operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}(x^*) = n+3.

This is the standard nonlinear observability rank condition.

D.3 Linearized Observability

For the linearized system at $\mathbf{X}^*$, the Kalman observability matrix is

\mathcal{O}_{lin} = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{H} \\ \mathbf{H}\mathbf{J}^* \\ \mathbf{H}(\mathbf{J}^*)^2 \\ \vdots \\ \mathbf{H}(\mathbf{J}^*)^{n+2} \end{bmatrix},

where $\mathbf{H} = \nabla_{\mathbf{X}} \mathbf{H}(\mathbf{X}^*)$. The linearized system is observable if $\operatorname{rank}(\mathcal{O}_{lin}) = n+3$.

Remark: Full rank of the linearized observability matrix is a sufficient condition for observability of the linearized system. It is not generally a sufficient condition for nonlinear local observability in every sense. The nonlinear condition requires the codistribution rank.

D.4 Extended Kalman Filter

The EKF linearizes along the current estimated trajectory:

\mathbf{J}_t = \frac{\partial \mathbf{F}}{\partial \mathbf{X}} \bigg|_{\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{t|t-1}}.

The state transition matrix over a small time step $\Delta t$ is approximated as

\boldsymbol{\Phi}_t = \exp(\mathbf{J}_t \Delta t).

Prediction step:

\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{t|t-1} = \hat{\mathbf{X}}_{t-1|t-1} + \int_{t-1}^t \mathbf{F}(\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{s|t-1}) ds,

\mathbf{P}_{t|t-1} = \boldsymbol{\Phi}_{t-1} \mathbf{P}_{t-1|t-1} \boldsymbol{\Phi}_{t-1}^\top + \mathbf{Q},

where $\mathbf{Q}$ is the process noise covariance.

Update step:

\mathbf{K}_t = \mathbf{P}_{t|t-1} \mathbf{H}_t^\top (\mathbf{H}_t \mathbf{P}_{t|t-1} \mathbf{H}_t^\top + \mathbf{R})^{-1},

\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{t|t} = \hat{\mathbf{X}}_{t|t-1} + \mathbf{K}_t (\mathbf{Y}_t - \mathbf{H}(\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{t|t-1})),

\mathbf{P}_{t|t} = (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{K}_t \mathbf{H}_t) \mathbf{P}_{t|t-1},

where $\mathbf{H}_t = \nabla_{\mathbf{X}} \mathbf{H}(\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{t|t-1})$.

Remark: The previous version incorrectly used a fixed Jacobian at $\mathbf{X}^*$ in the EKF. The corrected EKF linearizes along the estimated trajectory.

D.5 Candidate Observables

The latent variables $D, I, V$ are not directly observable. Candidate behavioral proxies include:

· Response latency for dependency $D$: Higher dependency correlates with longer hesitation during AI-free testing.
· Error correction rate for verification $V$: Users who verify AI outputs more frequently exhibit higher correction rates.
· Transfer performance for internalization $I$: Performance on novel tasks requiring the same underlying principles.
· Self-assessment accuracy for metacognition $M$: Calibration between predicted and actual performance.

The mapping from these observables to the latent state requires empirical calibration via regression or structural equation modeling.

